{
  "$description": "Core tokens (color, spacing, size, rules, components, layout) structured the way NYT ships them: the --tpl-* CSS custom properties on nytimes.com, read from the stylesheets of an Opinion guest essay (Oct. 2026). Names and tiers mirror the shipped CSS (--tpl-color-content-primary -> color.content.primary). NYT's source token files are not public, so the source format may differ; values and hierarchy are verified from the page. Modes: light/dark come from the space-toggle variables --tpl-pvt-light / --tpl-pvt-dark, switched by .tpl-user-default (follows prefers-color-scheme), .tpl-always-light, .tpl-always-dark and .tpl-user-inverse. This article sets .tpl-always-light. The 'article' group at the end is NOT NYT's: it's page-level values measured from this essay, aliased onto the NYT tokens. Size and spacing values are resolved for a 1440px-wide viewport (1440x900, root font 16px). Smaller-viewport values sit under $extensions.breakpoints. Type tokens live in opinion-photo-essay-type-tokens.json; load both files together. Dark-side mode values (dark, always-dark, user-inverse) are remapped to the Diatour artifact and the justus.works design system (dark theme); light and always-light values are NYT's, unchanged. Status reds and orange (negative, breaking, developing), static and overlay colors keep NYT's values because neither source system defines them.",
